{"head": "f12085a07377a3e5b63e8818432d620664ab8df1bf36ab0315134e52cc2b2e90", "collection": "events", "rows": [{"seq": 807, "time": "2026-10-27T14:45:47.902231+00:00", "kind": "acquisition_assessed", "payload": {"domain": "creator_objective_critique", "evidence": "source-516a0ab3340f436b", "outcome": "progress", "persistent_identifiers": [], "project": "proj-coc-01", "research_id": "immediate-3e4e056ab8f0e2b3", "route": "doi.org:source", "source_candidate_identities": {}, "source_candidates": [], "stage": "substantive_source"}, "prev_hash": "642baea3e901469576fb3e8dfaf565b4c60cdf1748c524fa28f7c784a3b159a0", "hash": "f214f1d470c230e1603db49a797e58fa8818b35aef041676ea342a2dc1936da2"}, {"seq": 806, "time": "2026-10-27T14:45:37.707780+00:00", "kind": "observation", "payload": {"actor": "collector", "content": "{\"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.513\", \"scope\": \"extracted web-page text; may be incomplete\", \"excerpt\": \"Detecting Proxy Gaming in RL and LLM Alignment via Evaluator Stress Tests - ACL Anthology\\nDetecting Proxy Gaming in\\nRL\\nand\\nLLM\\nAlignment via Evaluator Stress Tests\\nSanjeda Akter\\n,\\nIbne Farabi Shihab\\n,\\nAnuj Sharma\\nAbstract\\nProxy optimization, where AI systems exploit evaluator weaknesses rather than improve intended objectives, threatens both reinforcement learning (reward hacking) and LLM alignment (evaluator gaming). We introduce the Evaluator Stress Test (EST), an invariance-based framework that detects proxy gaming by separating exploitable sensitivity (e.g., format, physics bugs) from content-driven improvements using controlled perturbations with semantic validity audits. We validate EST across both domains. In RL, across 15 environments and 5 algorithms (2,156 expert-annotated episodes), EST achieves 78.4% precision and 81.7% recall. In LLM alignment, across 4 tasks, 2 model scales, 2 training methods, and 2 judges (1,200 human-annotated instances), EST achieves 74.2% precision and 78.6% recall with early warning signals preceding quality decline. Cross-domain analysis reveals that proxy-true correlation tracking transfers directly between domains, while perturbation design requires domain adaptation. Closed-loop mitigation improves human win-rate by 8.3 points (LLM) and reduces hacking by 54.6% (RL). We release benchmarks for both domains: 2,156 RL episodes and 1,200 LLM instances.\\nAnthology ID:\\n2026.findings-acl.513\\nVolume:\\nFindings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026\\nMonth:\\nJuly\\nYear:\\n2026\\nAddress:\\nSan Diego, California, United States\\nEditors:\\nMaria Liakata\\n,\\nViviane P. Moreira\\n,\\nJiajun Zhang\\n,\\nDavid Jurgens\\nVenue:\\nFindings\\nSIG:\\nPublisher:\\nAssociation for Computational Linguistics\\nNote:\\nPages:\\n10554–10583\\nLanguage:\\nURL:\\nhttps://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.513/\\nDOI:\\n10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.513\\nBibkey:\\nCite (ACL):\\nSanjeda Akter, Ibne Farabi Shihab, and Anuj Sharma. 2026.\\nDetecting Proxy Gaming in RL and LLM Alignment via Evaluator Stress Tests\\n. In\\nFindings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026\\n, pages 10554–10583, San Diego, California, United States. Association for Computational Linguistics.\\nCite (Informal):\\nDetecting Proxy Gaming in RL and LLM Alignment via Evaluator Stress Tests\\n(Akter et al., Findings 2026)\\nCopy Citation:\\nPDF:\\nhttps://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.513.pdf\\nChecklist:\\n2026.findings-acl.513.checklist.pdf\\nPDF\\nCite\\nChecklist\\nFix data\", \"excerpt_truncated\": false, \"source_sha256\": \"8974453a52206ae922179dd82bf5e803dc7ead3fb37c284e72deb8157456820e\", \"verification_required\": true, \"topic_domain\": \"creator_objective_critique\", \"evidence_role\": \"source\", \"host_tier\": \"verification-publisher\", \"source_identity\": \"doi:10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.513\", \"persistent_identifiers\": [], \"source_candidates\": [], \"source_candidate_identities\": {}}", "id": "source-51